WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
CodeQL is a bug bounty program like no other.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.000
It's because you don't actually get paid for finding the bug.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:14.000
You get paid for writing the scanner query to detect a vulnerability.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.000
And it is very intimidating at first.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.000
But for some reason, I've always felt like it's something good for me and for my skillset.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:36.000
Recently, I finally invested more time into it and I wrote my first proper CodeQL query to detect RCE vulnerability via ZipSlip.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.000
In this video, I will explain the query to you. Enjoy!

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:48.000
CodeQL has queries to detect many different kinds of vulnerabilities.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.000
But of course, no scanner is able to detect every single vulnerability.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:04.000
That's why GitHub, which is the owner of CodeQL, has GitHub Security Lab Bug Bounty Program, where they pay you for finding the query.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:11.000
So if you find a publicly known bug, a bug that has a CVE that's not detected by the scanner,

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:17.000
you can add the query to the scanner to detect this bug and GitHub will pay you for this.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:21.000
It can be a new bug, but it can also be old bug.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:25.000
It can be your bug, but it can be someone else's bug. It doesn't matter.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:31.000
What matters is that your query must make sense in general for other projects.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:38.000
You can't just write a query that's directed into detecting a single bug in a single application.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Nobody's going to pay for this.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:48.000
The query must be precise and must also detect this bug in other software.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:57.000
In my opinion, the hardest part of writing a CodeQL query is finding a bug that's not yet detected by CodeQL,

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:02.000
but you can reliably detect it by looking at the source code.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:07.000
Because some bugs you just can't model. How do you model a business logic error?

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:14.000
I don't think you can do it reliably, so it makes sense for all the software out there.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.000
And in this video, I will describe to you what the query does.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:25.000
But also in the last issue of BBRE Premium, I described the behind the scenes of writing the query,

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:31.000
how I found the bug to model, what bugs I wanted to model, but I couldn't for some reason,

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:38.000
why I made certain decisions, things I did to maximize payout. All those things are described in the article.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:43.000
If you are a BBRE Premium member, I will link this in the description down below.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:46.000
And if you're not, consider joining.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:55.000
The bug I decided to model was ZipSlip vulnerability in Discourse, which can lead to an RCE.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:57.000
How does it work?

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:03.000
Normally, when we download a zip file from the internet, we just double click it and opening

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.000
and extracting the files are the same operation.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:14.000
But in the code, it looks a bit different. First, you open the file that's an archive.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:20.000
Then, in the for loop, you access every single file from this archive.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:26.000
But before creating the file on the disk, you should verify if the path is secure.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:31.000
In this case, in the case of Discourse, this validation wasn't done.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:38.000
Using this ZipSlip vulnerability, you can create a file that has a dot dot slash sequence

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:44.000
in the file name. And then even though your file is inside the archive,

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.000
it will be unpacked to a completely different location.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:56.000
So this is an arbitrary file write vulnerability, which you can often turn into RCE in various ways.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.000
To detect any bug like this, we need three things.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:12.000
The first thing is the sink, which is a dangerous function to which malicious input should never go.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:16.000
And if it goes to this function, it means there's a vulnerability.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:24.000
Then we also need the source, which is a place where the server consumes our potentially malicious input

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:27.000
so we can send some payloads.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:35.000
Finally, we also need the flow from the source to the sink because the source itself or the sink itself

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:41.000
are not dangerous at all. And this is exactly how we'll write the CodeQL query.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:48.000
Let's start with the sink. You may think that the sink is the unpacking of the file,

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:56.000
but it's not the case. The other way to think about the sink is the last place in the source code

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:02.000
where I can look at it and say, OK, if my input gets to here, then I have the vulnerability

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:09.000
and it doesn't matter what happens later. It is much more connected with the impact of the bug.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:18.000
In this case, the impact is arbitrary file, right? So our sink will be file creation call.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:25.000
As you see, code review is a fundamental skill if you want to think about writing CodeQL queries

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:32.000
because first you have to understand where the bug happens in the code.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:39.000
To be more precise, the sink is not actually a function or a line. The sink is the path argument

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:45.000
of the file creation call. So in this case, it's the first argument.

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:50.000
You may ask, how about file contents? Do we somehow verify this as well?

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:57.000
The answer is we don't. We care about the file path and we assume that if we control the file path,

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:04.000
we probably also control the file contents. And even if we don't control file contents,

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:11.000
if we can write a file to an arbitrary location, even if the content is empty or whatever,

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:16.000
we can probably at least overwrite some files, leading to another vulnerability,

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:23.000
leading to denial of service. So in this case, we only care about the file path.

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:31.000
And this is the very simple definition of the sink in my query because I didn't have to model it from scratch.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:36.000
I used existing CodeQL models for this.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:44.000
We have the sink, so let's continue to model the source.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:50.000
The source should be the place where the server consumes the data from the attacker.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:58.000
In this case, however, it is just unpacking the file using one of the 3 libraries that I modeled

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:05.000
for working with archives. The logic is actually exactly the same in all three of them.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:12.000
And you may ask, if we consider this as a source, how do we know that this file actually comes

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:16.000
from the attacker and it's not a static file from the server?

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:23.000
And you're actually right. This query is prone to false positives because I only excluded the case

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:32.000
when it's called with a constant path. But it is still prone to situations where the server writes a file

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:40.000
that's created by the admin or is just on the server. So it isn't perfect, but it's as good as I could write it.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:48.000
I actually also described the exact reason in the article. Without getting into much details,

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:51.000
this is how it looks in the code.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:04.000
Okay, so we have the sink, we have the source. Now we need the third and final thing,

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:12.000
which is the flow of data from the source to the sink. And here we mostly rely on CodeQL doing its job

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:17.000
because CodeQL is not based on some simple regexes or something like this.

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:25.000
CodeQL knows, understands the code and knows how the data flows from one place to another.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:33.000
And mostly in the case of finding the flow from the source to the sink, we rely on CodeQL.

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:41.000
But in this case, it wasn't finding the bug. It was very frustrating because I had the bug just in front of me

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:49.000
and I had the query and it wasn't working on the real code base. I spent days here feeling like banging my head

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:55.000
against the wall, confirming if my sources are correct, if my sinks are correct.

00:08:55.000 --> 00:09:04.000
And it didn't make sense to me of why doesn't it work. But of course, turned out it made a lot of sense

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:13.000
because the thing is that CodeQL understands the code, but it's not magic. It's just some operations

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:20.000
are modeled in internal libraries that they allow the flow of the data from this place to this place.

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:29.000
But in this case, I'm working on custom classes like tarReaderEntry and the tarReaderEntry class

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:36.000
is not modeled inside CodeQL. So CodeQL doesn't know, so it doesn't understand it.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:45.000
And in case of this vulnerability, in every single case, the path was accessed using either the name

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:51.000
or a full name function call. And CodeQL just didn't know what these functions are,

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:58.000
so the flow was breaking in this step. But when we know where the flow breaks,

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:04.000
it's usually quite easy to counteract this because we have another predicate called

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:11.000
isAdditionalTaintStep where I can define exactly this. I can define the place where CodeQL thinks

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:18.000
the flow breaks, but it actually doesn't break. So I just defined that the call to a function

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:26.000
called fullName or name does not break the flow. Although this isn't yet the end of the job,

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:32.000
my query finally was finding the vulnerability in discourse in the real code base,

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:38.000
which made me really happy and relieved after days of frustration and disappointment

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:42.000
because I thought that maybe I just won't be able to model this.

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:52.000
It's not yet the end of the job because it's important for my query to find the bug when it's there,

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:57.000
but it's also important that my query doesn't find the bug when it's not there

00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:04.000
because if there is a sanitization before creating the file, we should also detect this

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:12.000
and tell CodeQL that the flow should break there. We basically do the reverse of what we did previously.

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:19.000
Previously, the flow was breaking and we had to tell CodeQL that this operation does not break the flow.

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:25.000
In this case, the CodeQL has the full flow and we have to tell it that this operation

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:30.000
is probably sanitizing the file path, so the flow should break there.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:35.000
I used the existing definition of sanitizer from the path traversal query,

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:40.000
but it wasn't enough to find the way the bug was fixed in discourse.

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:47.000
So I also created another definition of sanitizer that matched how the bug was fixed in discourse.

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:58.000
The only problem is it doesn't work at all. It would detect this fix if it was inline check,

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:02.000
but it doesn't detect it when it's a separate function call.

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:06.000
And to be honest, to this day, I don't know why this happens.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:12.000
And if you know, just hit me a message because I'm really, really curious.

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:19.000
But despite this, GitHub Security Lab awarded me five and a half thousand dollars for this query,

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:24.000
which made me really, really happy because it is my highest bounty.

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:31.000
If you want to improve in CodeQL, maybe for getting bug bounty from GitHub Security Lab,

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:37.000
but personally, I also start using it as a semi-automatic code review tool.

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:43.000
If you're interested in CodeQL, I honestly recommend you to check out BBRE Premium.

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:50.000
I write things that I would have liked to hear before because I just know how overwhelming CodeQL is

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:55.000
and how underwhelming is the amount of articles out there.

00:12:55.000 --> 00:13:03.000
And if you want to improve your code review skills, first check out this video about my XSS in Golang.

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:20.000
For now, thank you so much for watching and goodbye.

